# The total statement coverage this repo will not go below, as a percentage.
#
# It is a RATCHET, not a target. Raising it is a deliberate committed act — you
# edit this file, in the change that earned the increase, and a reviewer sees
# the number move. Nothing raises it automatically, because a floor that
# follows the measurement is not a floor: it makes today's number the rule and
# leaves the next drop with nothing to hit.
#
# Nor is it a goal. Coverage did not catch a single one of the four ordering
# defects that motivated issue #171 (a data race on a fake's exported field, a
# scheduler that parked forever, tests that passed only under -race, an archive
# adoption window) — statement coverage does not measure ordering, which is why
# the soak job exists beside this. What the floor buys is narrow and real: the
# number cannot slide quietly while the suite grows.
#
# scripts/coverage-ratchet.sh allows a 0.5pp dip below this line before it
# fails, because the total moves by a tenth or two on unrelated changes — a
# refactor that deletes covered code, a new error path in a rarely-hit branch —
# and a gate that reddens on noise gets ignored, then removed.
#
# THE NUMBER IS THE CI RUNNER'S, NOT YOUR MACHINE'S, and they differ by around
# a point. The gate runs on a clean runner with none of the external engines
# installed (no PipeWire, whisper, piper, kokoro), so the tests that probe for
# them skip and their code goes uncovered; a development box has some of those
# and reports a higher total. Measured on the same commit: 83.4% locally,
# 82.2% on ubuntu-latest. The floor has to be the number the gate measures, or
# it fails on the day it lands — which is exactly what it did. If your local
# reading is a point above this line, that is the reason, and it is not a
# licence to raise it: run the ratchet job's number, or read it off a CI run.
#
#   make coverage-ratchet          # measure and compare
#   make coverage-ratchet-raise    # print the line to paste here, if it is time
#
# 2026-08-29, issue #173 (the store fault-injection suite): 82.2 → 82.7.
# Measured as a delta rather than read off a runner, because the increase was
# earned locally and the local:CI gap is a constant of the missing engines,
# which this change does not touch: the same commands read 83.3% on this
# machine before the change and 83.8% after, so the runner's number moves by
# the same half a point. The tolerance covers the estimate — a CI reading
# anywhere at or above the old 82.2 still passes — and the whole half point
# comes from internal/conversations going 66.4% → 91.0%.
82.7
